We found that the dual-circular ROA arises from the axial octupolar order, which is a representative example of multipolar order [Fig. 1(a)], and provides a direct and symmetry-sensitive signature of their order parameters. In contrast to conventional probes, which rely on the high-momentum-transfer or external fields to break multipolar symmetry, our analysis demonstrates that the cross-circular ROA arises under spatially uniform photoelectric fields and can therefore be of experimentally measurable magnitude. The quantitative significance is evidenced by the first-principles calculations of the cross-circular ROA of pyrite, a prototypical material hosting the axial octupolar anisotropy.

Furthermore, the phonon excitations relevant to the proposed cross-circular ROA exhibit a characteristic three-dimensional motion, which can be viewed as a three-dimensional realization of chiral phonons [51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56, 57, 58]. We found that the _multipolar phonon_ plays the vital role in the cross-circular ROA of axial octupolar materials. Our work not only provides a convenient optical probe of the multipolar orders, which have been regarded as 'hidden' due to the scarcity of the external stimuli coupled directly to them, but also implies the intriguing connection between the multipole physics and chiral phonon.

_Results--_ We begin by considering the symmetry condition for dual-circular ROA. The following symmetry analysis focuses on the cross-circular ROA defined as \(U_{\rm CC}=I_{\rm LR}-I_{\rm RL}\), although a similar discussion applies to the parallel-circular ROA defined by \(U_{\rm PC}=I_{\rm LL}-I_{\rm RR}\). Supposing the backscattering geometry with the normal incidence of light along a \(n\)-fold (\(n>2\)) rotation axis, which eliminates spurious birefringence effect [59, 60, 61], we obtain the following relation imposed by the out-of-plane mirror operation \(m_{\perp}\) whose mirror plane contains the light beam axis:

\[U_{\rm CC}(\delta\omega,\omega,\mathcal{O})=-U_{\rm CC}(\delta\omega,\omega,m _{\perp}\mathcal{O}). \tag{1}\]

Since the mirror operation interchanges the circular polarization of light, the sign of \(U_{\rm CC}\) is flipped.

The dependence on physical quantities, such as the crystal structure and order parameter, is denoted by \(\mathcal{O}\). If \(m_{\perp}\mathcal{O}=\mathcal{O}\) holds, cross-circular ROA is forbidden because \(U_{\rm CC}(\delta\omega,\omega,\mathcal{O})=0\). The symmetry for \(m_{\perp}\) is broken by axial dipoles or chirality, which respectively preserve and break space-inversion symmetry [62]. For instance, the magnetization \(\mathbf{M}\) parallel to the incident light is inverted under the \(m_{\perp}\) operation: \(m_{\perp}\mathbf{M}=-\mathbf{M}\).

In the following, we consider the octupolar order belonging to the irreducible representation \(A^{\tau}_{2g}\) of the cubic point group \(m\bar{3}m\) where \(\tau=\pm\) represents the time-reversal (\(\theta\)) parity. In context of the multipolar degrees of freedom in solids [63, 64], \(A^{+}_{2g}\) and \(A^{-}_{2g}\) orders correspond to the \(xyz\)-type axial and magnetic octupolar orders [Fig. 1(a)]. This type of octupolar anisotropy neither breaks the cubic symmetry nor realizes the chirality, but violates the symmetry regarding the mirror operation \(m_{\perp}\) perpendicular to \(\{111\}\) and crystallographically-equivalent planes. Conversely, the \(m_{\perp}\) operation inverts the polarity of the axial octupolar order [Fig. 1(b)]. As a result, the cross-circular ROA can occur when the light is incident on these planes. From the perspective of crystallographic groups, taking the high-symmetric cubic point group \(m\bar{3}m1^{\prime}\) for the para-state, the ordered states characterized by \(m\bar{3}1^{\prime}\) (in the \(A^{+}_{2g}\) case) and \(m\bar{3}m^{\prime}\) (in the \(A^{-}_{2g}\) case) exhibit cross-circular ROA distinct from those observed in axial dipolar and chiral materials [65].

Next, we demonstrate cross-circular ROA through a microscopic analysis. The Raman scattering intensity is given by the semiclassical formula [66], such as

\[I_{\rm LR}(\delta\omega,\omega)=\left|\mathbf{e}_{\rm R}^{\dagger}\hat{\alpha}( \delta\omega,\omega)\mathbf{e}_{\rm L}\right|^{2}, \tag{2}\]

where the right circularly polarized incident light and left circularly polarized scattered light are given by the polarization vectors \(\mathbf{e}_{\rm R}=(1,i,0)/\sqrt{2}\) and \(\mathbf{e}_{\rm L}=(1,-i,0)/\sqrt{2}\), respectively. The Raman tensor \(\hat{\alpha}\) for an elementary excitation is composed of the excitation \(\Phi\) and the nonlinear susceptibility \(\hat{\chi}^{\Phi}\), expressed as \(\hat{\alpha}\equiv\Phi(\delta\omega)\hat{\chi}^{\Phi}(\delta\omega,\omega)\). The nonlinear susceptibility is defined by the relation

\[P_{a}(\omega+\delta\omega)=\sum_{b}\chi^{\Phi}_{ab}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi( \delta\omega)E_{b}(\omega), \tag{3}\]

where \(a,b=x,y,z\). The formula describes the correction to the electric susceptibility induced by \(\Phi\) with a frequency shift \(\delta\omega\).

The cross-circular ROA originates from the excitations \(\Phi_{1}\) and \(\Phi_{2}\), which belong to the complex irreducible representations \(E^{1}_{g}\) and \(E^{2}_{g}\) of \(m\bar{3}\). The symmetry-adapted form of \(\chi^{\Phi}_{ab}\) for each excitation is given by

\[\hat{\chi}^{(1)}=\chi_{1}{\rm diag}\left(1,\xi^{2},\xi\right),\hat{\chi}^{(2)} =\chi_{2}{\rm diag}\left(1,\xi,\xi^{2}\right), \tag{4}\]

where \(\xi=\exp(2\pi i/3)\)1. The Raman tensors represent dynamics characterized by sequential stretching and contraction, occurring in the order of [100]-[001]-[010] directions and [100]-[010] directions [65]. Intriguingly, those dynamics represent the three-dimensional extension of the chiral phonons [51, 40, 55]. More explicitly, while the eigenmode of the chiral phonon is defined in a plane, the \(E^{1,2}_{g}\)-mode dynamics cannot be reduced to that in a plane or line, and thus the corresponding displacement may be called a multipolar phonon.

The cross-circular Raman scattering intensities for the light incidence along [111] are obtained as

\[I_{\rm LR}^{[111]}(\delta\omega,\omega) =\left|\chi_{1}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{1}(\delta\omega)\right|^{2}, \tag{5}\] \[I_{\rm RL}^{[111]}(\delta\omega,\omega) =\left|\chi_{2}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega)\right|^ {2}, \tag{6}\]

and hence we obtain the corresponding ROA as

\[U_{\rm CC}^{[111]}=\left|\chi_{1}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{1}(\delta\omega) \right|^{2}-\left|\chi_{2}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega)\right|^{2}, \tag{7}\]

Thus, the cross-circular ROA arises from differences in the magnitudes of \(\hat{\chi}^{(1,2)}\) and \(\Phi_{1,2}\). Furthermore, the octupolar anisotropy and the multipolar phonon give rise to cross-circular ROA whose sign alternates depending on the incident plane. For instance, \(\chi_{1}\) and \(\Phi_{1}\) contribute to \(I_{\rm LR}\) for the light incidence along [111], while they contribute to \(I_{\rm RL}\) for the incidence along \([\bar{1}11]\). For the latter case, the ROA is given by

\[U_{\rm CC}^{[111]}=\left|\chi_{2}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega) \right|^{2}-\left|\chi_{1}(\delta\omega,\omega)\Phi_{1}(\delta\omega)\right|^ {2}, \tag{8}\]

equal to \(-U_{\rm CC}^{[111]}\). As illustrated in Fig. 1(c,d), the alternating signs of ROA reflect the octupolar configuration of the axial vectors penetrating the faces of the octahedron.

Let us now consider the \(\theta\)-even case, that is \(A_{2g}^{+}\) octupolar system. In phonon-mediated Raman scattering, the degenerate multipolar phonon modes \(\Phi_{1,2}(\delta\omega)\) are equally excited in equilibrium because of the \(\theta\) symmetry, such that \(\left|\Phi_{1}(\delta\omega)\right|=\left|\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega)\right|\). Consequently, the cross-circular ROA for the \(\{111\}\) face, \(U_{\rm CC}^{[111]}(\delta\omega,\omega)\), occurs by the imbalance between the magnitudes of nonlinear susceptibilities \(\left|\chi_{1,2}(\delta\omega,\omega)\right|^{2}\).

We demonstrate the multipolar cross-circular ROA by considering the full set of spinless \(d\)-orbital fermions residing on a primitive cubic lattice. The tight-binding Hamiltonian is given by \(H=\sum_{\mathbf{k}}H_{0}(\mathbf{k})+H_{\rm ax}(\mathbf{k})\). \(H_{0}(\mathbf{k})\) shows the cubic symmetry (\(m\bar{3}m\)) and is parametrized using the Slater-Koster method. The axial multipolar term \(H_{\rm ax}(\mathbf{k})\), governed by the single parameter \(t_{\rm ax}\), is derived from the multipole-based analysis [67; 68]. Coupling to the external fields, such as the electric field and phonon excitations, is also included to evaluate the nonlinear susceptibility. The electromagnetic perturbation is treated via the minimal coupling between the momentum and vector potential as \(\mathbf{p}\to\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{A}\). We note that the electromagnetic fields are treated with the electric-dipole approximation, and hence no spatial dispersion of light is taken into account. The multipolar phonon excitations \(\Phi_{1,2}\) are introduced through the perturbative Hamiltonian

\[\delta H_{\Phi}=\sum_{\mathbf{k}}\Phi_{1(2)}\,\mathbf{c}_{\mathbf{k}}^{\dagger}{\rm diag}( 1,\xi^{\pm 1},\xi^{\mp 1})\mathbf{c}_{\mathbf{k}}, \tag{9}\]

where the creation operators of \(t_{2g}\) manifold denotes \(\mathbf{c}_{\mathbf{k}}^{\dagger}=\left(d_{yz}^{\dagger},d_{zx}^{\dagger},d_{xy}^{ \dagger}\right)\)2. Further details of the model are provided in Ref. [65].

Footnote 2: Although the operator \(\delta H_{\Phi}\) is non-hermitian, the present calculation is linear in \(\Phi_{1,2}\) and therefore does not suffer from the non-hermitian property due to the superposition.

The nonlinear susceptibilities \(\chi_{1,2}\) are microscopically computed by the established perturbative approach [65].

Figure 2: Hopping from \(d_{z^{2}}\) to the perturbed \(d_{yz}\) state, defined in the coordinate system with \(X=[1\bar{1}0]\) and \(Z=[111]\) in (a) and with \(X^{\prime}=[110]\) and \(Z^{\prime}=[\bar{1}11]\) in (b). The hopping process is decomposed into \(d_{z^{2}}\to d_{yz}\) and \(d_{z^{2}}\to\pm d_{zx}\), which result from the original (\(H_{0}\)) and axial octupolar (\(H_{\rm ax}\)) terms, respectively. The hybridized \(\left|d_{zx}\right\rangle\) state stems from the \(\pm\pi/2\) rotation of the original \(\left|d_{yz}\right\rangle\) state mediated by the face-dependent axial dipoles.

The numerical calculations confirm that the obtained susceptibilities are adapted to each irreducible representation as \(\chi_{1}\equiv\chi_{xx}^{(1)}=\xi\chi_{yy}^{(1)}=\xi^{-1}\chi_{zz}^{(1)}\) and \(\chi_{2}\equiv\chi_{xx}^{(2)}=\xi^{-1}\chi_{yy}^{(2)}=\xi\chi_{zz}^{(2)}\), consistent with Eq. (4). Figure 3(a) presents the dependence of \(|\chi_{2}|^{2}\) and \(|\chi_{1}|^{2}\) on the incident light frequency \(\omega\). It is evident that the two susceptibilities differ significantly from each other, and the difference \(|\chi_{1}|^{2}-|\chi_{2}|^{2}\) reverses sign when the octupolar parameter \(t_{\rm ax}\) is inverted. In Fig 3(b), we show the normalized difference \(\text{CC}_{\chi}\equiv\left(|\chi_{1}|^{2}-|\chi_{2}|^{2}\right)/\left(|\chi_ {1}|^{2}+|\chi_{2}|^{2}\right)\). The dichroism is particularly pronounced in the presence of the resonant particle-hole excitations (\(\omega\gtrsim 1.2\)), similar to the ROA observed in the ferroaxial [45] and antisymmetric Raman scattering [69; 70].

Let us corroborate the physical origin of the cross-circular ROA. The \(E_{g}^{1}\) and \(E_{g}^{2}\) multipolar phonons show the circular motion in the \(\{111\}\) and its equivalent planes, thereby coupled to the electron's orbital rotation induced by the axial octupolar order. To elucidate the orbital rotation, we analyze the hopping symmetry between the \(d_{x^{2}},d_{yz}\) orbitals with the cartesian coordinates defined by the \(X=[1\bar{1}0]\) and \(Z=[111]\) [Fig. 2(b)]. The nearest-neighbor hopping parameter for the non-octupolar part is given by \(t_{\alpha}(\mathbf{k})\equiv\bra{d_{yz}}H_{0}(\mathbf{k})\,|\,d_{z^{2}}\rangle\), while that for the axial octupolar part is by \(t^{\prime}_{\alpha}(\mathbf{k})\equiv\bra{d_{yz}}H_{\rm ax}(\mathbf{k})\,|\,d_{z^{2}}\rangle\) [Fig. 2(a)]. The obtained multipolar hopping is related to the nearest-neighbor hopping concerning the \(d_{zx}\) orbital as \(t^{\prime}_{\alpha}(\mathbf{k})\propto\text{sign}(t_{\rm ax})\cdot t_{\beta}(\bm {k})\) (\(t_{\beta}(\mathbf{k})\equiv\bra{d_{zx}}H_{0}(\mathbf{k})\,|\,d_{z^{2}}\rangle\)) [65]. The similarity between \(t^{\prime}_{\alpha}\) and \(t_{\beta}\) is attributed to the perturbed state \(\ket{d^{\prime}_{yz}}=\ket{d_{yz}}+\delta\ket{d_{zx}}\) (\(\delta\propto t_{\rm ax}\)) by which the hopping process \(d_{z^{2}}\to d_{zx}\) is admixed with the \(d_{z^{2}}\to d_{yz}\) hopping. The hybridization between \(\ket{d_{yz}}\) and \(\ket{d_{zx}}\), denoting the orbital rotation along the \(Z\) axis, is consistent with nature of the axial dipole pointing to \(Z=[111]\), as the axial dipole relate the quantities in a transversal manner [71]. The orbital rotation leads to the cross-circular ROA through its coupling to the circular motion of the \(E_{g}^{1,2}\) phonons. This physical picture has been supported by the analytical calculations for a ferroaxial system [72; 45].

Owing to the octupolar order, the axial dipoles and the associated orbital rotations alternate between the faces of the octahedron. For example, in the coordinate system defined by \(X^{\prime}=[110]\) and \(Z^{\prime}=[\bar{1}11]\), the \(\ket{d_{yz}}\) state undergoes the opposite orbital rotation as described by \(\ket{d^{\prime\prime}_{yz}}=\ket{d_{yz}}-\delta\ket{d_{zx}}\) [Fig. 2(b)]. Consequently, the cross-circular ROA exhibits its incident-plane dependence in agreement with the symmetry analysis.

Cross-circular ROA is similarly realized in the \(A_{2g}^{-}\) (\(\theta\)-odd) octupolar order. The magnetoaxial octupolar term of \(H_{\rm ax}\) is expressed by the orbital-current order which reduces the point group symmetry as \(m\bar{3}m1^{\prime}\to m\bar{3}m^{\prime}\)[65]. As in the \(\theta\)-even case, supposing the phonon excitation \(\Phi_{1,2}\) in Eq. (3), we observe that the nonzero \(\text{CC}_{\chi}\) shows the sign reversal under inversion of the \(A_{2g}^{-}\) multipolar order [Fig.3(c)].

Moreover, in addition to the imbalance in nonlinear susceptibilities, the \(A_{2g}^{-}\) order lifts the degeneracy of the \(\Phi_{1,2}(\delta\omega)\) multipolar phonons, giving rise to the different \(\delta\omega\) dependence [Eq. (7)]. The \(\theta\)-odd nature allows for the energy gain phenomenologically given by

\[\delta E_{\Phi}(\delta\omega)=\eta_{\rm ax}\,\delta\omega\left[\left|\Phi_{1} (\delta\omega)\right|^{2}-\left|\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega)\right|^{2}\right], \tag{10}\]

where \(\eta_{\rm ax}\) (\(\propto t_{\rm ax}\)) stands for the coupling between the \(A_{2g}^{-}\) order and the multipolar motion of phonons expressed as \(\mathbf{\Phi}\times\partial_{t}\mathbf{\Phi}\) for \(\mathbf{\Phi}=(\text{Re}\Phi_{1},\text{Im}\Phi_{1})=(\text{Re}\Phi_{2},-\text{Im} \Phi_{2})\). Thus, the resonant frequencies of \(\Phi_{1,2}(\delta\omega)\) are slightly shifted in a staggered manner as \(\delta\omega_{\pm}=\Omega_{0}\pm\delta\Omega\) where \(\delta\Omega\propto\eta_{\rm ax}\) as in the case of electrons' energy levels [73]. As a result, the Raman tensor \(\hat{\alpha}(\delta\omega_{+},\omega)\) is predominantly determined by \(\Phi_{1}(\delta\omega_{+})\), while \(\hat{\alpha}(\delta\omega_{-},\omega)\) is dominated by \(\Phi_{2}(\delta\omega_{-})\), pointing to the cross-circular ROA for \(\delta\omega=\delta\omega_{\pm}\) without compensation of the contributions of \(\Phi_{1}\) and \(\Phi_{2}\). In contrast, for the \(\theta\)-even case, the modes \(\Phi_{1,2}(\delta\omega)\) remain degenerate due to no \(\theta\)-odd coupling \(\eta_{\rm ax}=0\).

Intriguingly, the Zeeman-like splitting of Eq. (10) leads to the contrasting Stokes and anti-Stokes signals, which are the Raman scattering processes involving the creation and annihilation of phonons. The mode \(\Phi_{2}(-\delta\omega_{+})\) plays a dominant role in \(\hat{\alpha}(-\delta\omega_{+},\omega)\), while \(\Phi_{1}(-\delta\omega_{-})\) does in \(\hat{\alpha}(-\delta\omega_{-},\omega)\) as evident from the prefactor \(\delta\omega\) of Eq. (10). Thus, the cross-circular ROA \(U_{\rm CC}(\pm|\delta\omega|,\omega)\) stemming from the \(A_{2g}^{-}\) octupolar order shows the antisymmetry between the Stokes (\(-|\delta\omega|<0\)) and anti-Stokes (\(+|\delta\omega|>0\)) signals, since each signal is primarily governed by either of the \(\Phi_{1}\) or \(\Phi_{2}\) excitation [62]. The antisymmetry is similarly found in the \(\theta\)-odd axial dipolar order (_e.g.,_ ferromagnetic order) [38].

On the other hand, the \(A_{2g}^{+}\) octupolar state shows the Stokes and anti-Stokes cross-circular scattering intensities with the same signs, because the nonlinear susceptibilities do not show significant change between \(|\chi_{i}(\delta\omega,\omega)|\) and \(|\chi_{i}(-\delta\omega,\omega)|\) for \(i=1,2\) under the condition \(|\delta\omega|\ll|\omega|\)[65]. It follows that the careful comparison of Stokes and anti-Stokes signals provides a means to identify the time-reversal parity of the multipolar order [74, 75, 76, 77, 62].

Finally, we present the first-principles analysis of the cross-circular ROA of pyrite (FeS\({}_{2}\)), demonstrating that the multipolar ROA attains a substantial magnitude. Its crystal structure is labeled by the space group \(Pa\bar{3}\) (No. 205) whose point group \(m\bar{3}1^{\prime}\) is of our interest. In Fig. 4, we plot the Raman spectrum and the normalized ROA \(\mathrm{CC}_{\chi}=\mathrm{CC}_{\chi}(\omega)\). In agreement with the preceding discussions, the cross-circular ROA is observed in the \(E_{g}^{1,2}\) modes at \(\delta\omega=332\) (cm\({}^{-1}\)). The facet-dependent ROA is also consistent with our predictions [Fig. 4(a)]. Importantly, the ROA is sizable as much as \(\mathrm{CC}_{\chi}\) amounting to several ten percents [Fig. 4(a)]. The quantitative significance is promising for the detection of the multipolar order and is consistent with the experimental result [78].

_Discussions--_ We proposed the dual circular ROA originating from the axial octupolar order. By integrating the symmetry analysis and microscopic calculations, we have demonstrated the emergence of cross-circular ROA in the \(A_{2g}^{+}\) (\(\theta\)-even) and \(A_{2g}^{-}\) (\(\theta\)-odd) axial multipolar phases. This activity enables all-optical measurement of multipolar orders and further suggests its potential as a tabletop probe of multipolar order, and indicates the close relation with chiral phonon. The close relation further implies that, by combing the circular-light driving of multipolar phonons [79, 55], one can realize the all-optical write and read protocol for the axial octupolar order.

Though the quantitative significance has been demonstrated by our first-principles calculations, further material-specific investigations are highly desirable. For instance, the \(\theta\)-odd type is expected in the all-in-all-out spin state of pyrochlore magnet, noncoplanar spin state of rare-earth galium (aluminum) garnets, and the \(\theta\)-odd octupolar state of spin-orbit-coupled systems [9, 22, 27, 80, 81, 82, 83]. For the \(\theta\)-even case, to the best of our knowledge, no experimental report has been made on the axial octupolar ordering resulting in the symmetry reduction from \(m\bar{3}m1^{\prime}\to m\bar{3}1^{\prime}\). The phase transition of interest might be realized in the rare-earth oxides \(R_{2}\)O\({}_{3}\) (\(R\) : rare earth) or double perovskites, where the crystal structures can be tuned via pressure or chemical substitution [84, 85]. Further exploration of the octupolar dual-circular ROA in real materials is an important direction for future research.

